<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Aden Settlement</h1><p class="st-meta">1839 CE – 1937 CE · capital Aden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Aden as a dependency of British India, seized in 1839 to serve as a coaling station on the steamship route to Bombay.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In January 1839 forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> under Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford-bettesworth-haines">Stafford Haines</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-lahej">Sultanate of Lahej</a> after a dispute over a plundered ship. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> wanted a coaling station for steamships between Suez and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, and Aden was governed from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bombay-presidency">Bombay Presidency</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford-bettesworth-haines">Haines</a> served as political agent and built up the port, which had shrunk to a village. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> was declared a free port in 1850, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> opened in 1869 it became one of the world's busiest bunkering stations, with Indian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>, Jewish and Yemeni communities. From the 1870s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> signed protection treaties with the hinterland rulers, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>. Its administration passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> Act of 1935, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> on 1 April 1937, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-aden">Colony of Aden</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aden-settlement.jpg" alt="Aden Settlement" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Barryob · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Raj_Red_Ensign.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1839 CE – 1937 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stafford Bettesworth Haines</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, with Hindu, Jewish and Christian minorities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aden Province, British Aden (1839-1937)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=aden-settlement" aria-label="Aden Settlement on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5916 1701 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aden Settlement lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6236 1957L6247 1946M6236 1957L4997 651M6236 1957L7364 1602M6236 1957L6236 1957"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6247 1946h0M4997 651h0M7364 1602h0M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6236 1957h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=aden-settlement">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
